<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Transport policy for the seller app.

     WHY THIS EXISTS
       The seller credential is a long-lived device secret sent as
       `x-orderak-secret` on every request. It has no expiry and no rotation, so
       a single successful interception yields an indefinitely valid credential.
       That design is defensible on its own terms — shared.ts argues correctly
       that stretching a 122-bit random token buys nothing — but it does mean the
       transport is the only thing standing between an attacker and permanent
       account access. Pinning is the control that matches that threat.

       Declaring the base config also makes the cleartext policy explicit rather
       than inherited. targetSdk 36 already defaults it off; stating it means a
       future targetSdk change cannot quietly re-enable it.

     WHY THE ROOTS AND NOT THE LEAF OR THE INTERMEDIATE
       The leaf rotates roughly every 90 days, so pinning it would brick the app
       four times a year. The intermediate (Google Trust Services WE1 today)
       rotates less often but is still operational infrastructure Cloudflare
       replaces without notice. The roots below are the stable anchors — GTS
       Root R4 terminates the live chain for orderak.app and staging.orderak.app,
       and GTS Root R1 is Google's other widely deployed root, included as the
       backup pin RFC 7469 requires so a single rotation cannot lock everyone out.

       Both values were computed from the live chains rather than copied:
         openssl s_client -connect orderak.app:443 -servername orderak.app -showcerts
         openssl x509 -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
           | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl enc -base64
       Both match Google Trust Services' published SPKI pins.

     THE RISK THIS CARRIES, STATED PLAINLY
       orderak.app is fronted by Cloudflare, and Cloudflare chooses the CA. It
       uses Google Trust Services today; it can move to Let's Encrypt or DigiCert
       without telling anyone. If that happens while a pinned build is in the
       field, every request fails and the only remedy is a Play release — which
       users in Egypt on low-end devices may not take promptly.

       `expiration` is the safety valve and is not optional. After that date
       Android ignores this pin set entirely and falls back to normal CA
       validation, so the failure mode is "pinning stops protecting" rather than
       "the app stops working". Renewing it is a deliberate act: re-run the
       commands above, confirm the chain still terminates at one of these roots,
       and move the date. See docs/runbooks/certificate-pinning.md.

     The mock flavour is unaffected: app/src/mock/AndroidManifest.xml scopes
     `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` to itself for the 10.0.2.2 emulator host, and
     the domain-config below only covers orderak.app. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">orderak.app</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2027-06-30">
            <!-- GTS Root R4 — terminates the current orderak.app chain
                 (leaf CN=orderak.app -> GTS WE1 -> GTS Root R4). -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">mEflZT5enoR1FuXLgYYGqnVEoZvmf9c2bVBpiOjYQ0c=</pin>
            <!-- GTS Root R1 — backup pin. Not in today's chain; present so a
                 rotation within Google Trust Services does not require a release. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">hxqRlPTu1bMS/0DITB1SSu0vd4u/8l8TjPgfaAp63Gc=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
